#4
Background Field Estimation may blur some other key contrast details in attempt to highlight few.
Plane and Radial Fit Algorithm may fail because of many features that contadict the idea of uniform ascent/ descent.
* Local Contrast and Saturation Counting are additions that can be used for CLAHE (for fail cases)
Colour and Haze Signature is data intense 

#4.1
Logic: Estimate the smooth background/ illumination field and normalize the image against it.
Methods:
Plane Fit: models directional brightness variation.
Radial Fit: models center-to-edge/ vignetting variation.
Risk: Background estimation may incorrectly suppress low-contrast retinal features → potential loss of microaneurysms, vessels, hemorrhages, etc.
Optimisation: Estimate the illumination field at reduced resolution and apply correction to the original image. Only apply at places with severely low/ high illumination.
Fails: Significant loss of local contrast/ detail after correction → retain original image. If whole image is of form severely low/ high illumination then exit.

#4.2
Logic: Divide image into local tiles → histogram equalization → clip excessive histogram peaks → redistribute contrast.
Colour approach: Prefer a luminance/detail channel:
Green (G): strong retinal-detail baseline.
Local Contrast: Measures whether local retinal features become more distinguishable after CLAHE.
Saturation Count: Detects excessive enhancement/clipping at intensity extremes.
Optimisation: Apply only to Borderline images and primarily within the retinal/FOV region. Apply to certain elements (like blood vessels with high R value)
Risk: Excessive CLAHE can amplify noise/artifacts or suppress/distort subtle DR features.
Fails: Significant increase in saturation/noise or decrease in sharpness/detail → revert to original or use milder CLAHE parameters.
